values ('draft-pilot-g6-social-geography-early-humans', 'bridge', '6', 'social-studies', 'Map the Move: Why Communities Settle', 'Use geographic evidence to explain why early communities settled in particular places and how those choices shaped daily life.', 'Historical Thinking, Geography, and Early Humans', '["c3"]'::jsonb, 'How can a map help us explain how people use a place?', 'Geography creates possibilities and constraints. Historical reasoning explains how people used evidence, resources, and tradeoffs to make choices.', 'This matters because learners use historical thinking, geography, and early humans to explain, build, predict, compare, or solve beyond one quiz.', '["source","community","evidence"]'::jsonb, '["Say what you already know about this social studies idea.","Point to one example or clue before answering.","Explain the first step in your own words."]'::jsonb, '{"warmUp":"Choose between two map locations and write one question you would need answered before settling.","directInstruction":"People use geography as one source of evidence. Resources, routes, hazards, choices, and adaptations all shape community life.","guidedPractice":"Match map features to possible needs, benefits, risks, and adaptations.","interactiveActivity":"Use the interactive settlement board to spend evidence tokens and revise the choice after a new hazard appears.","independentPractice":"Write a claim about the best settlement location using two map clues and one tradeoff.","reteachPath":"Label one map feature, connect it to one human need, and add a because sentence.","challengePath":"Defend a less obvious location by showing how technology or adaptation changes the tradeoff."}'::jsonb, '[{"title":"Look","note":"Separate what the map shows from what historians infer."},{"title":"Build","note":"Require at least two benefits and one tradeoff before accepting a settlement claim."},{"title":"Explain","note":"Avoid saying geography makes people act one way; ask what choices and adaptations were possible."}]'::jsonb, '[{"misunderstanding":"Treating a map symbol as proof of a whole historical explanation.","repair":"Name the visible feature, then explain the reasonable inference and its limit.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Listing resources without connecting them to daily life.","repair":"Complete: This resource could support ___ because ___.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Assuming one location has only benefits or only risks.","repair":"Add a tradeoff and explain how a community might adapt.","signal":""}]'::jsonb, '[{"placement":"lesson-hero","title":"Settlement decision map","description":"A labeled river-valley map connects water, fertile soil, transportation, hazards, and a community decision.","prompt":"Create a bold educational hero image for Map the Move: Why Communities Settle. Show the concept clearly with no real children, no logos, and no copyrighted characters.","altText":"A settlement map labels a river, fertile soil, travel route, flood risk, and a community decision supported by evidence."},{"placement":"teaching-diagram","title":"Map the Move: Why Communities Settle diagram","description":"Resource: Water, soil, animals, and materials can support daily life. Route: Rivers and landforms can make travel, trade, or communication easier. Tradeoff: The same place can offer benefits and hazards, such as fertile soil and flooding.","prompt":"Create a labeled diagram for Map the Move: Why Communities Settle with 3-5 readable callouts and high contrast.","altText":"Diagram showing Resource, Route, Tradeoff."},{"placement":"ai-tutor","title":"Map the Move: Why Communities Settle tutor help card","description":"Write whether you are stuck reading the map, connecting a resource to daily life, weighing a tradeoff, or writing the historical claim.","prompt":"Create a small tutor help card for Map the Move: Why Communities Settle that shows one visual hint and one retry step.","altText":"Tutor visual hint for Map the Move: Why Communities Settle."}]'::jsonb, '[{"questionText":"Which pair is a geographic benefit and a tradeoff?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["River water and possible flooding","A title and a homework grade","A symbol and a random guess","A story character and a theme"],"correctAnswer":"River water and possible flooding","explanation":"A river can support life and travel while also creating flood risk.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"social-studies-historical-thinking-geography-and-early-humans","standardTag":"c3"},{"questionText":"What makes a settlement claim stronger?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["It uses at least two map clues and explains the connection","It uses the longest sentence","It names only one resource","It ignores hazards"],"correctAnswer":"It uses at least two map clues and explains the connection","explanation":"Evidence plus reasoning makes the historical claim testable and understandable.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"social-studies-historical-thinking-geography-and-early-humans","standardTag":"c3"},{"questionText":"Which statement avoids overclaiming?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["The river forced everyone to settle there","The river may have supported water and travel, while flooding required adaptation","Maps prove exactly what every person believed","Resources always create the same society"],"correctAnswer":"The river may have supported water and travel, while flooding required adaptation","explanation":"The statement uses cautious language and includes both opportunity and adaptation.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"social-studies-historical-thinking-geography-and-early-humans","standardTag":"c3"}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"standard-c3","title":"c3 standards tag","url":"","claim":"Map the Move: Why Communities Settle is aligned to c3; state-specific mapping remains data-driven before district rollout.","checkedAt":"2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z","reviewerNote":"Internal standards tag reviewed for the pilot publication bundle."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"Settlement council","roles":["Map analyst","Resource historian","Risk checker","Adaptation designer","Council speaker"],"sharedArtifact":"Submit an annotated settlement map and a claim-evidence-reasoning briefing with one tradeoff and adaptation.","individualAccountability":"Each learner explains one part in their own words."}'::jsonb, 'published', '', 'Published core pilot lesson reviewed for student-facing app-led instruction, visual support, tutor prompts, and mastery checks.', 'Includes alt text, short chunks, keyboard-friendly tasks, high-contrast SVG support, and non-color-only instructions.', 'bridge lesson uses age-fit pacing, vocabulary, visuals, active learning, and retry supports.', true, 'ready', '[]'::jsonb, 'g6-social-geography-early-humans', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 5, '[]'::jsonb, false, 'approved', '{}'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 0, '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z', '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z')
