values ('draft-pilot-g6-learning-ai-build-test', 'bridge', '6', 'computer-science', 'Learning AI', 'Explain core AI terms, describe frontend and backend responsibilities, write useful AI prompts, and use a bug-testing loop on a small app idea.', 'Learning AI, Building Apps, And Testing Projects', '["k12cs"]'::jsonb, 'How can I use AI like a responsible builder instead of treating it like magic?', 'AI is not magic: it is one tool inside a human-led build, test, and review process.', 'This matters because learners use learning ai, building apps, and testing projects to explain, build, predict, compare, or solve beyond one quiz.', '["algorithm","debug","pattern"]'::jsonb, '["Say what you already know about this computer science idea.","Point to one example or clue before answering.","Explain the first step in your own words."]'::jsonb, '{"warmUp":"Look at two AI prompts. Choose which one is more useful and explain why.","directInstruction":"AI tools can generate suggestions from prompts and context. A builder still decides the goal, protects private information, checks the output, and tests the project. The frontend is the visible user experience. The backend stores data, runs rules, and connects services through APIs.","guidedPractice":"Sort cards into AI terms, frontend parts, backend parts, tool/agent boundaries, and testing actions. Then revise one weak prompt into a stronger builder prompt.","interactiveActivity":"Use the AI Builder Map to connect prompt -> AI output -> frontend screen -> backend API -> database -> approved tool call -> test -> bug fix.","independentPractice":"Design a tiny study-helper app. List what the learner sees, what the backend stores, what AI helps with, what tool boundary protects users, and three tests before launch.","reteachPath":"Use the restaurant metaphor: frontend is what customers see, backend is the kitchen/order system, and AI is a helper that still needs a manager.","challengePath":"Write a prompt that asks AI to find bugs in a project, then critique the AI''s answer with a test checklist, privacy check, accessibility check, and launch checklist."}'::jsonb, '[{"title":"Look","note":"Do not let students paste private names, addresses, passwords, or personal records into AI tools."},{"title":"Build","note":"Require students to define done before asking AI for help: audience, goal, constraints, and success checklist."},{"title":"Explain","note":"Have students test AI output with small examples, edge cases, and a plain-language bug report."},{"title":"Recall","note":"Teach students that API keys, database URLs, and passwords belong in environment variables, not in public code or prompts."},{"title":"Hint 5","note":"When an AI agent uses a tool, require a permission, cost, log, and human-review checkpoint before trusting the result."}]'::jsonb, '[{"misunderstanding":"Thinking AI always knows the truth.","repair":"Teach that AI predicts useful text and can be wrong. Factual claims need source checks or tests.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Thinking frontend and backend are the same thing.","repair":"Use a restaurant metaphor: frontend is the menu and table service; backend is the kitchen, inventory, and order system.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Asking AI vague questions and accepting the first answer.","repair":"Use a prompt checklist and ask for alternatives, assumptions, tests, and possible bugs.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Testing only the happy path.","repair":"Check normal cases, weird inputs, missing data, permission mistakes, mobile layout, keyboard use, and whether the bug comes back.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Forgetting that apps need launch and maintenance.","repair":"Teach that real projects need environment variables, deployment checks, logs, error reports, backups, and a plan for user feedback.","signal":""},{"misunderstanding":"Thinking AI agents can safely use every tool automatically.","repair":"Teach tool boundaries: the agent needs permission, limited access, cost controls, logs, and a human review gate for important actions.","signal":""}]'::jsonb, '[{"placement":"lesson-hero","title":"AI builder system map","description":"A neon flow map showing student idea, prompt, AI model, frontend, backend, database, tests, bug reports, and final project.","prompt":"Create a bold educational hero image for Learning AI. Show the concept clearly with no real children, no logos, and no copyrighted characters.","altText":"A diagram connects a student''s goal to a prompt, AI model output, frontend screen, backend server, database, tests, and bug fix loop."},{"placement":"teaching-diagram","title":"Learning AI diagram","description":"Prompt and context: A prompt tells the AI the role, goal, constraints, examples, and output format. Context is the information the AI can use. Frontend and backend: The frontend is what the user sees and clicks. The backend handles data, rules, accounts, APIs, and storage. Test and debug loop: Good builders reproduce the bug, isolate the cause, fix one thing, retest, and write down what changed. Agent and tool boundary: AI agents and tools need permission limits, cost limits, logs, and human review before they can affect a real project.","prompt":"Create a labeled diagram for Learning AI with 3-5 readable callouts and high contrast.","altText":"Diagram showing Prompt and context, Frontend and backend, Test and debug loop, Agent and tool boundary."},{"placement":"ai-tutor","title":"Learning AI tutor help card","description":"Write the exact part that is confusing: AI definitions, prompt writing, frontend, backend, API, database, agents, tool calls, testing, debugging, safety, costs, logs, or checking if the AI is wrong.","prompt":"Create a small tutor help card for Learning AI that shows one visual hint and one retry step.","altText":"Tutor visual hint for Learning AI."}]'::jsonb, '[{"questionText":"Which prompt is most useful for getting AI help?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["You are helping a Grade 6 student. Explain APIs with a restaurant metaphor, give one example, and ask one check question.","Make it better.","Do my project.","Tell me everything about computers."],"correctAnswer":"You are helping a Grade 6 student. Explain APIs with a restaurant metaphor, give one example, and ask one check question.","explanation":"The best prompt gives role, audience, goal, format, and a clear definition of useful output.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"computer-science-learning-ai-building-apps-and-testing-projects","standardTag":"k12cs"},{"questionText":"In a web app, which part is usually frontend work?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["The buttons and screens the user sees","The private database password","The server rule that checks permissions","The stored account records"],"correctAnswer":"The buttons and screens the user sees","explanation":"Frontend work is the visible interface: screens, buttons, forms, layout, and user interactions.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"computer-science-learning-ai-building-apps-and-testing-projects","standardTag":"k12cs"},{"questionText":"What should you do before trusting an AI-generated answer or code change?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["Test it and check important claims","Assume it is always correct","Paste private passwords so it has context","Skip debugging if it sounds confident"],"correctAnswer":"Test it and check important claims","explanation":"AI can be useful and still wrong. Builders test outputs, verify important claims, and protect private data.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"computer-science-learning-ai-building-apps-and-testing-projects","standardTag":"k12cs"},{"questionText":"Which bug report is strongest?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["When I click Start, the lesson page stays blank. I expected the lesson to open. Steps: sign in, go to Child, click Start.","It is broken.","The app is bad.","Fix this now."],"correctAnswer":"When I click Start, the lesson page stays blank. I expected the lesson to open. Steps: sign in, go to Child, click Start.","explanation":"A useful bug report says what happened, what was expected, and the steps to reproduce it.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"computer-science-learning-ai-building-apps-and-testing-projects","standardTag":"k12cs"},{"questionText":"What is the safest way to let an AI agent use a paid image tool in a school app?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["Give it a clear task, cost limit, content rules, log, and human approval before publishing","Let it generate unlimited images without review","Give it every password so it has more context","Publish the first image immediately if it looks cool"],"correctAnswer":"Give it a clear task, cost limit, content rules, log, and human approval before publishing","explanation":"Real AI tools need boundaries: permission, cost controls, safety checks, logs, and review before students see the result.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"computer-science-learning-ai-building-apps-and-testing-projects","standardTag":"k12cs"}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"standard-k12cs","title":"k12cs standards tag","url":"","claim":"Learning AI is aligned to k12cs; state-specific mapping remains data-driven before district rollout.","checkedAt":"2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z","reviewerNote":"Internal standards tag reviewed for the pilot publication bundle."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"AI builder crew","roles":["Prompt engineer","Frontend designer","Backend mapper","Bug hunter","Safety reviewer"],"sharedArtifact":"Submit a one-page AI app blueprint with a prompt, screen sketch, backend/API map, agent/tool boundary, test checklist, bug report, and safety rule.","individualAccountability":"Each learner explains one part in their own words."}'::jsonb, 'published', '', 'Published core pilot lesson reviewed for student-facing app-led instruction, visual support, tutor prompts, and mastery checks.', 'Includes alt text, short chunks, keyboard-friendly tasks, high-contrast SVG support, and non-color-only instructions.', 'bridge lesson uses age-fit pacing, vocabulary, visuals, active learning, and retry supports.', true, 'ready', '[]'::jsonb, 'g6-learning-ai-build-test', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 5, '[]'::jsonb, false, 'approved', '{}'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 0, '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z', '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z')
