values ('draft-next-wave-scholar-9-english-9-u1-l1', 'scholar', '9', 'ela', 'Short stories: 1. English 9 learning mission', 'Use short stories ideas to read, annotate, discuss evidence, and publish a short response.', 'Short stories', '["ccss-ela"]'::jsonb, 'How does short stories help us understand or solve a real problem?', 'This next-wave lesson turns short stories into an app-led learning mission with a visual model, a student explanation checkpoint, and a delayed recall reward.', 'Learners use short stories to explain something observable, solve a real problem, or build an artifact instead of only answering a worksheet.', '["main idea","detail","evidence"]'::jsonb, '["Ready to notice examples of short stories","Can explain a first attempt in plain words"]'::jsonb, '{"warmUp":"The app opens with a quick notice-and-wonder prompt: look at a story map, claim-evidence organizer, or vocabulary image, then write one thing you notice and one question you have.","directInstruction":"The app teaches the core idea in three short beats: concrete example, visual model, then academic language. The student must explain the idea back before moving on.","guidedPractice":"The student solves a low-risk practice item with hints. The tutor asks what feels confusing before giving a strategy.","interactiveActivity":"The student completes the active task: course task, seminar or lab evidence, portfolio artifact, and transfer problem. The screen captures a drawing, sorting choice, model, or written explanation.","independentPractice":"The student answers two mastery checks and one transfer question that changes the context.","reteachPath":"If mastery is below threshold, the app switches to a simpler visual, fewer steps, and a first-principles prompt.","challengePath":"If mastery is strong, the app asks the learner to create a new example, explain a misconception, or connect the skill to another subject."}'::jsonb, '[{"title":"Notice first","note":"Start with what the learner notices before naming the academic rule."},{"title":"Explain before scoring","note":"Ask for an explanation in the learner''s words before scoring mastery."},{"title":"Recall for reward","note":"Schedule retrieval after the lesson so rewards depend on retention."}]'::jsonb, '[{"misunderstanding":"The learner may retell details without naming the central idea or claim.","repair":"Ask the student to point to the exact part of the visual that supports their answer before using symbols or academic wording.","signal":"The answer is correct-looking, but the explanation cannot connect to the model."}]'::jsonb, '[{"placement":"lesson-hero","title":"Short stories visual model","description":"story map, claim-evidence organizer, or vocabulary image","prompt":"Create a bold, high-contrast classroom-safe diagram for grade 9: story map, claim-evidence organizer, or vocabulary image. No real children, no tiny text, simple labels, app UI friendly.","altText":"Diagram support for Short stories: 1. English 9 learning mission"},{"placement":"teaching-diagram","title":"Short stories step-by-step diagram","description":"A labeled teaching diagram that breaks short stories into visible parts, examples, and one student action.","prompt":"Create a clean teaching diagram for grade 9 ela: Use short stories ideas to read, annotate, discuss evidence, and publish a short response.. Include short labels, arrows, one example, and a learner action. No real children, no logos, no tiny text.","altText":"Step-by-step diagram for Short stories: 1. English 9 learning mission"}]'::jsonb, '[{"questionText":"Which explanation best shows the idea of short stories?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["A memorized fact only","A model plus a reason","A guess with no evidence","A copied definition only"],"correctAnswer":"A model plus a reason","explanation":"Mastery means the learner can connect the answer to a model or evidence, not just repeat a phrase.","difficultyLevel":"core","skillTag":"ela-short-stories","standardTag":"ccss-ela"},{"questionText":"What should the tutor ask first when the learner is stuck?","questionType":"multiple-choice","choices":["What answer do you want?","What exactly feels confusing?","Should I skip this?","Do you want the final answer?"],"correctAnswer":"What exactly feels confusing?","explanation":"The tutor should diagnose the stuck point before choosing an explanation mode.","difficultyLevel":"transfer","skillTag":"tutor-confusion-diagnosis","standardTag":"ccss-ela"}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"next-wave-k12-production","title":"Internal K-12 production scope map","url":"","claim":"Lesson is generated from the full curriculum production map and must pass manager, truth, visual, and accessibility review before publication.","checkedAt":"","reviewerNote":"Review standards fit, lesson clarity, visual usefulness, and age-appropriate task design before publishing."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"Short stories crew mission","roles":["3-5 learner team with project lead, researcher, analyst, designer, and reviewer"],"sharedArtifact":"Create a shared visual artifact that explains short stories and includes one individual reflection per teammate.","individualAccountability":"Each learner explains one part in their own words."}'::jsonb, 'review', '', 'Next-wave production draft 5 of 9. It is intentionally review-gated until manager, truth, visual, and accessibility checks pass.', 'Requires alt text, readable labels, keyboard-completable interaction, text alternative for any diagram, and no color-only instructions.', '9-12 pacing and task structure. Foundation lessons use parent-supported play; Bridge and Scholar lessons use group/product evidence.', true, 'ready', '[]'::jsonb, null, '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 4, '["Manager and Truth And Fact-Check review required before publication."]'::jsonb, false, 'needs-human-review', '{}'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 0, '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z', '2026-07-10T12:00:00.000Z')
